proofs.proof_tex:
The upper lemma supplies one finite Borel estimator, namely the branch-defined and clipped \(\widehat\tau_n(x_0)\).  Therefore the definition of minimax risk and \(\mathrm{lem:closed\mbox{-}regime\mbox{-}fixed\mbox{-}point\mbox{-}upper}\) give
\[
 R_n\le\sup_{P\in\mathcal P(d,\alpha,\beta,\gamma,L)}
 \mathbb E_{P_O^{\otimes n}}|\widehat\tau_n(x_0)-\tau_P(x_0)|
 \le C\rho_n.                                                       \tag{37}
\]

For the converse, take the two finite priors from
\(\mathrm{lem:closed\mbox{-}regime\mbox{-}fixed\mbox{-}point\mbox{-}component\mbox{-}mixtures}\), let \(\mathsf M_+,\mathsf M_-\) be their genuine fixed-\(n\) observed-data mixtures, and let their constant target values be \(\theta_+,\theta_-\).  Put \(\Delta=|\theta_+-\theta_-|\ge c_0\rho_n\).  If \(m_+,m_-\) are densities relative to a common dominating measure, Cauchy--Schwarz gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
 \|\mathsf M_+-\mathsf M_-\|_{\mathrm{TV}}
 &=\frac12\int|\sqrt{m_+}-\sqrt{m_-}|(\sqrt{m_+}+\sqrt{m_-})\\
 &\le\frac12H(\mathsf M_+,\mathsf M_-)
 \left\{\int(\sqrt{m_+}+\sqrt{m_-})^2\right\}^{1/2}
 \le H(\mathsf M_+,\mathsf M_-)\le\frac12.                         \tag{38}
\end{aligned}
\]
Fix an arbitrary Borel estimator \(T\), and test the two mixtures by selecting the target value nearer to \(T\), with deterministic tie breaking.  A testing error implies \(|T-\theta_\varepsilon|\ge\Delta/2\).  Consequently,
\[
\begin{aligned}
 \sup_{P\in\mathcal P}\mathbb E_P|T-\tau_P(x_0)|
 &\ge\frac12\sum_{\varepsilon\in\{+,-\}}
       \int\mathbb E_P|T-\theta_\varepsilon|\,d\Pi_\varepsilon(P)\\
 &\ge\frac\Delta4\{\mathsf M_+(\varphi=-)+\mathsf M_-(\varphi=+)\}\\
 &\ge\frac\Delta4\{1-\|\mathsf M_+-\mathsf M_-\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\}
 \ge\frac{c_0}{8}\rho_n.                                          \tag{39}
\end{aligned}
\]
The penultimate inequality is the variational definition of total variation applied to the acceptance set.  Taking the infimum over all Borel \(T\) proves the lower bound.  The priors are supported on the exact-uniform full-support Bernoulli class, so (39) concerns the stipulated fixed-\(n\) product experiment.  If any broader model contains this exact class and uses the same point target \(\tau_P(x_0)\), then the supremum over that broader class is at least the supremum over this subclass, so the lower bound transfers upward.  Equation (37) was proved only for the exact-uniform class and makes no upper-bound claim for a broader model.

Combining (37)--(39) yields \(R_n\asymp\rho_n\).  At \(S=S_\star\), direct substitution gives
\[
 \eta=\left(1+\frac d{2\gamma}+\frac{2\gamma+d}{2\gamma}\right)^{-1}
 =\frac\gamma{2\gamma+d},
\]
so the same-class oracle power \(n^{-\gamma/(2\gamma+d)}\) is attained with no logarithmic multiplier.  If \(S<S_\star\), the same algebra gives \(\eta<\gamma/(2\gamma+d)\), and hence
\(R_n/n^{-\gamma/(2\gamma+d)}\asymp n^{\gamma/(2\gamma+d)-\eta}\to\infty\).  Thus, within the stipulated closed regime, oracle order is attained exactly at the elbow.
